  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/1CCC81">Definition <span data-tag="1CCC81">6.2.1.5</span>.</a>localization!topologicaltopological!localizationtopological!class of morphisms Let $\calX $ be a presentable $\infty $-category, and let $\overline{S}$ be a strongly saturated class of morphisms of $\calX $. We will say that $\overline{S}$ is <em>topological</em> if the following conditions are satisfied: </p>
  <p><ul>
  <li class="custom-item" data-marker="$(1)$"><p>
There exists $S \subseteq \overline{S}$ consisting of <em>monomorphisms</em> such that $S$ generates $\overline{S}$ as a strongly saturated class of morphisms. 
</p>
</li><li class="custom-item" data-marker="$(2)$"><p>
Given a pullback diagram 
</p>
<div class="equation">
  \[  \xymatrix { X' \ar [r] \ar [d]^{f'} &amp;  X \ar [d]^{f} \\ Y' \ar [r] &amp;  Y } \]
</div>
<p>
 in $\calX $ such that $f$ belongs to $\overline{S}$, the morphism $f'$ also belongs to $\overline{S}$. 
</p>
</li>
</ul></p>
  <p>We will say that a localization $L: \calX \rightarrow \calY $ is <em>topological</em> if the collection $\overline{S}$ of all morphisms $f: X \rightarrow Y$ in $\calX $ such that $Lf$ is an equivalence is topological. </p>
